687 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409687 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409687 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc16361c-3306-5054-a003-e31388cab6e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409688 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409688 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409688 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409688 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(B)=-7.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409688 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a80ad4c-5529-5931-9d7c-574d40c6f47e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409688 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $2,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $2$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $2$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =2\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-2det(B) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409692 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409692 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f93ad19-15c5-55b8-98ea-d5bcee8f0a3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409692 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2aef95ee-e7e9-5cac-b9ad-3b67a53db79f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409694 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409694 #edb/ref 1062 #uui